{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 380,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Audible for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3250,
    "dDurationMs": 4809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum computing is cool, but you know what\nwould be extra awesome - a quantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8060,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact if we want the first we’ll need\nthe latter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12470,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the first step to the quantum internet\nis quantum cryptography."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22500,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum theory may seem like an obscure subject\nof questionable relevance to the average person."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27919,
    "dDurationMs": 4811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in fact much of our technological world\ndepends on our understanding of the quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32730,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties of the subatomic universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35250,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And soon, perhaps very soon, we’ll be interacting\nwith the weirdness of quantum mechanics even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41270,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more directly – with the coming of quantum\ncomputing and the quantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46430,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to talk about the latter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48810,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Specifically quantum cryptography and quantum\nkey distribution – the foundations of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55110,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prospective quantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56940,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We may come back to quantum computers in detail\n– but for now just a word on why their advent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62010,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will demand a quantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64040,
    "dDurationMs": 6429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The logic gates of a quantum computer exist\nin a state of quantum superposition of many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70469,
    "dDurationMs": 2371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simultaneous configurations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72840,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This allows the iterations of certain types\nof calculation to be done in parallel, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78420,
    "dDurationMs": 2909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vastly more quickly than in a classical computer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81329,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a quantum computer can calculate\nthe prime factors of large numbers extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86719,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87859,
    "dDurationMs": 5561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is bad because prime factoring is a cornerstone\nof internet cryptography."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93420,
    "dDurationMs": 5329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the best description of classical internet\ncryptography … on the internet … head"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98749,
    "dDurationMs": 2471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Infinite Series."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101220,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By comparison, my summary will be pathetic,\nbut here it is: To send encrypted emails or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107800,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "credit card details two parties need to share\na cryptographic key."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112400,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can be as simple as a number that you\nneed in order to unscramble a message."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118609,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most secure way to do this would be to\nmeet under a bridge on a rainy night to swap"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123509,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a key in advance – a so-called private key."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127329,
    "dDurationMs": 3951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s impractical, so we use public keys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131280,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most widespread example is the RSA protocol."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135000,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Choose two prime numbers - one of which is\nlarge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137580,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Multiply them together to get an even larger\nnumber and broadcast that as your public key."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144220,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyone can then send you encrypted messages\nby scrambling the message with the public"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149420,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key using a special one-way function."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152860,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a mathematical process that can't be\nundone with the public key - only with its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158130,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prime factors, which only you have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161110,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of this works as long as the public key\ncan't be easily factorized back into its two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166940,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "primes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167940,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is another type of one-way function - it's\nmuch, much harder to factorize large numbers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173650,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than it is to create them by multiplication\nin the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177650,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least as long as we're limited by classical\ncomputing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181850,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once quantum computers can factorize public\nkeys quickly the entire public key system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187460,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "falls apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188570,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two levels of snooping that encryption\nshould prevent: simple eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195320,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attacks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196720,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eavesdropping is just someone reading some\nof your message data without you noticing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200160,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a MITM attack someone fully intercepts\nand controls a communication stream, essentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206740,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impersonating both parties to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209350,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The latter is especially dangerous because\nthe attacker could potentially control the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sharing of a public key, inserting their own\nkey in the middle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218480,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Entire new levels of authentication are required\nto fix this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222650,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course you could just meet under a bridge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225280,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A metaphorical quantum-mechanical bridge,\nwhich allows the sharing of a secure private"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231630,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232630,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Enter quantum key distribution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235710,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a scheme to allow the generation of\na shared private key, which can be used with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241060,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a traditional encryption algorithm, or as\na one-time-pad which encrypts each message"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247040,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a one-use key."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249290,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While QKD doesn’t really solve the authentication\nproblem, it does make undetected eavesdropping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256370,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257370,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "QKD is actually two different schemes – at\nleast that we’ll look at today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261630,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each highlights a different fundamental weirdness\nof quantum mechanics – the Heisenberg uncertainty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266510,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle and quantum entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269500,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These will be the keys to unbreakable\ncryptography of a quantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274170,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start with Heisenberg, which of course\nwe’ve done an episode on already."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278860,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The uncertainty principle tells us that we\ncannot simultaneously know the values of certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283160,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs of properties – for example, a particle’s\nposition and momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287860,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are so-called complementary or conjugate\nvariables."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292000,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another example is the polarization of a photon,\na quantum of electromagnetic wave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298570,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Polarization defines the direction that its\nelectric and magnetic fields … wave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303110,
    "dDurationMs": 6550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can either measure the vertical-versus-horizontal\npolarization OR the diagonal-versus-other-diagonal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309660,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "polarization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311460,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rectilinear and diagonal polarization are\ncomplementary properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314860,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Measure one perfectly and you lose all information\nabout the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319360,
    "dDurationMs": 6059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An unmeasured photon exists in a state of\nmaybe-vertical maybe-horizontal – a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325419,
    "dDurationMs": 3211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the two, with maybe a preference for one\nor the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328630,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also exists in a superposition of diagonal\nstates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332760,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can measure the state by, for example,\nsending the photon through a polarizing filter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339020,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That act forced the photon to make a choice\n– first which basis – rectilinear or diagonal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345770,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– then which actual direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348580,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So for example, pass a randomly-polarized\nphoton through a horizontal polarization filter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355169,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a polarized sunglasses lens and the photon\nwill either decide it’s vertically polarized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361160,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and be blocked or horizontal and get through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366180,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Run that now-horizontally-polarized photon\nthrough a vertical filter and it’ll be blocked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371800,
    "dDurationMs": 7610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because its vertical polarization component\nhas been measured to be zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379410,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Try it with two polarized sunglasses lenses\nat 90-degrees – all light gets blocked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386970,
    "dDurationMs": 1129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact here’s an quantum experiment you\ncan do at home."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388099,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Place a third polarization filter in between\nthe two at a 45-degree diagonal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393340,
    "dDurationMs": 3079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bizarrely, now some light gets through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396419,
    "dDurationMs": 1271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What did you just do?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397690,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You just switched between different quantum\nrepresentations of reality and then back again,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403900,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so invoked the uncertainty principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406520,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, any photon coming out of the first filter\nhas its rectilinear polarization perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412260,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measured, which means its diagonal polarization\nis completely undefined."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418400,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when it reaches that diagonal filter it\nhas a 50-50 chance of passing through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423750,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then its diagonal polarization is perfectly\ndefined, making its rectilinear polarization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429930,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "undefined so it has a 50-50 chance to traverse\nthat third vertical filter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436310,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the basis of one of the first quantum\nkey distribution algorithms developed in 1984"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441770,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bennett and Brassard and known\nas BB84."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445980,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And no, no astromech droids were named after\nthis key distribution protocol."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451430,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which works like this:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453729,
    "dDurationMs": 6291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine we have Alice and Bob – no, Albert\nand Niels - who want to decide on a private"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460020,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key for their messages, but don’t want to\nmeet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463760,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Albert generates a random string of bits,\n0’s and 1’s and encodes these bits using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468889,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons polarized in a particular basis, and\nuses a randomly chosen basis, either rectilinear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475699,
    "dDurationMs": 2511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or diagonal, for each of the photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478210,
    "dDurationMs": 5299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These bits are then sent over an open channel\nto Niels who then randomly picks a basis of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483509,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his own for each photon and projects onto\nthat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487480,
    "dDurationMs": 5249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If he uses the same basis that Albert did,\nhe gets the same result, i.e. Albert sent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492729,
    "dDurationMs": 6921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a 1 and Bob also gets a1, otherwise he will\nget a random result – Albert sends a 1 but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499650,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Niels will get a 1 or 0 with equal probability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502620,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over that same public channel they randomly\npick a subset of those bits and Albert reveals"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508550,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which basis was used for those photons, and\nwhat she sent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513500,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If Niels used a different basis he ignores\nthe result because he knows it will be random."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519690,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If he used the same basis he should get the\nsame answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524029,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If he doesn’t, they know something was up\n- and this is where the quantum part becomes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530020,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "useful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531020,
    "dDurationMs": 7650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If someone... not Eve ... Werner, intercepted\nthese messages, which were after all sent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538670,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a public channel, and did his own measurements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He will have disturbed the system in a way\nthat Albert and Niels could detect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546640,
    "dDurationMs": 6199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because Werner, like Niels, can only\npick a random basis each time on which to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552839,
    "dDurationMs": 2071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project the photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554910,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If he picks the right one, the photon state\nis unchanged, otherwise it will project the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560250,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon onto a random state, meaning Niels\nisn’t guaranteed to get the same answer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566170,
    "dDurationMs": 4419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as Albert, even though they know they used\nthe same basis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570589,
    "dDurationMs": 4141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this way, Albert and Niels can detect a\nman in the middle attack."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574730,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They would know that someone had knocked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577120,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once they have done this test and verified\nthat the information wasn’t intercepted,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582589,
    "dDurationMs": 4161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they discard the non-matching measurements\nand keep the rest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586750,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each translates to a bit – 1 or 0 depending\non the basis choice – rectilinear or diagonal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592930,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gives a number that becomes their private\nkey, which now only they know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598580,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s possible for Werner to get lucky and\nguess the exact same basis as Albert and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604500,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not disturb the state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606580,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the chance is 1 in 2 to the power of the\nnumber of photons, which quickly gets close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612720,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to impossible given that Werner only\ngets one shot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617410,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This makes eavesdropping on the channel impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621610,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Man-in-the-middle attacks are in principle\nstill possible because Werner could impersonate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626360,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Albert and Niels from the very start."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629450,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are classical authentication methods\nthat can make this very difficult."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634520,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s BB84, and it's one path to a secure\nquantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640880,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to generate secure keys using\nquantum mechanics was developed by Artur Ekert"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645800,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 1991."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646840,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It uses a similar choice-of-quantum-basis\nmechanism but with the added frill of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653090,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654430,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it looks for violations of Bell's\ninequality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658730,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here we definitely have to direct you to our\nfull episode on quantum entanglement for the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663740,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "details."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664740,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the super-brief summary: create a pair\nof particles with a quantum property that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669680,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is correlated between the two – for example,\nelectrons with opposite spin axes or photons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676540,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with 90-degree polarizations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678520,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our choice of measurement direction will set\nthe diection of those spin or polarizations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684010,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "axes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685010,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s where the entanglement comes in:\nchoose an axis or basis on which to measure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691180,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of those particles – say up-down for\nspin or rectilinear for polarization - and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696680,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other also becomes “measured” in that\nbasis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701380,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It will have the opposite when measured in\nthe same basis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705820,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if measured in a different basis\nto its entangled partner, the results of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712410,
    "dDurationMs": 4299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurements will be correlated in a way that\ndepends on the choice of measurement basis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716709,
    "dDurationMs": 2171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at both ends."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718880,
    "dDurationMs": 3769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gives us another way to transmit a secure\nkey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722649,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This time Albert creates a set of entangled\nparticle pairs and transmits one half of those"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728610,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs to Niels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729610,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He then chooses a set of bases to measure\nhis own particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734680,
    "dDurationMs": 4159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The choice of basis represents a set of bits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738839,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Niels chooses a random set of bases to measure\nthe particles he receives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743910,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because they’re entangled, the outcome of\nAlberts and Niels measurements should be correlated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749480,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a very particular way defined by Bell’s\ntheorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753810,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If not then they know that the message was\ntampered with by someone, even if they can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759769,
    "dDurationMs": 1061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say their name."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760830,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it was probably that Werner dude – he\nmust have made some measurements and disentangled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766430,
    "dDurationMs": 2029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particles en route."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768459,
    "dDurationMs": 7111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If Bell’s theorem is satisfied, the entanglement\nremained intact, no tampering occurred."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775570,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Albert and Niels can create a private key\nbased on the basis choices that happened to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781390,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "match, just like with BB84."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783640,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, the classical internet is still moderately\nsafe – or at least it’s biggest vulnerability"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790890,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is human stupidity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793220,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which to be fair will still be the case in\na quantum internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796470,
    "dDurationMs": 7470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But come quantum computers even the smartest\nclassical security protocols will be compromised."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803940,
    "dDurationMs": 7230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then we’ll also need a quantum internet\n… which we know how to do in theory, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811170,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s a different matter to actually build\none."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814580,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum states – and particularly entangled\nstates – are notoriously fragile and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819700,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s hard to transmit them across large\ndistances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822180,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll show you how to construct a vast,\nplanet-spanning network of encrypted quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828420,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states real soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830690,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your browsing history may one day depend on\nit, and I assume that history isn’t just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836240,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "old episodes of Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838500,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Audible for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841120,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Audible’s selection of audiobooks includes\nAudible Originals, audio titles created by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846360,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "storytellers from around the literary world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849250,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, you can check out Bill Byrson’s\nApplication of science, where Bill tells the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "story of scientific instruments that created the first\nmap of Britain and the device that measures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860440,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the temperature of the sea from space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863250,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s an exciting narrative that explores\nthe importance of collaboration in the field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868180,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of scientific discovery."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870690,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Visit audible.com/spacetime OR text spacetime\nto 500-500 to learn more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877640,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Members own their books and can access them\nanytime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880920,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more, visit audible.com/spacetime\nOR text spacetime to 500-500."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886910,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we're covering comments from the last\ntwo videos - our episode on the galaxy without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891630,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter, and our coverage of the event\nhorizon telescope's black hole image."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896430,
    "dDurationMs": 1230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's get to it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897660,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AspLode asks about the interaction between\ndark matter and black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901490,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do they interact?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902630,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can dark matter form a black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904780,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well assuming that dark matter is some sort\nof exotic particle - which is the going hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909500,
    "dDurationMs": 5709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- then black holes would definitely attract\ndark matter gravitationally, and occationally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915209,
    "dDurationMs": 1451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eat the stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916660,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not too much of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919090,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of dark matter's definig qualities is\nthat it doesn't clump together - it remains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923880,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffusely spread out through our galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927690,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Occasional dark matter particles would be\nsnared by black holes - and they would add"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931709,
    "dDurationMs": 3751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to its mass just like regular matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935460,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But dark matter alone could never clump together\ndensely enough to produce a black hole by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940970,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941990,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Munrais asks whether putting radio telescopes around the sun would improve the resolution of an interferometer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948990,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hell yeah it would."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950280,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The minimum resolvable distance is inversely\nproportional to the separation of the antennas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956740,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tommy Barlow states that nor the event horizon,\nnor anything near it, was imaged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961780,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah, That's right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962780,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The event horizon is invisible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965020,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the telescope DID resolve down to the\nsize of the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969880,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What was imaged was light escaping from the\nphoton sphere that was produced a the magnetically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975580,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "driven jet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976740,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's probably around 5 times larger than\nthe event horizon, in the case of this quasar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981260,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But seriously, the \"light from outside the\nevent horizon because we can't actually see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986040,
    "dDurationMs": 4419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon telescope\" doesn't have\nsuch a nice ring to it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990460,
    "dDurationMs": 8180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Inebriati logs the following: Day 37,736:\nEinstein still appears to be right about general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998640,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I know, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999780,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At what point do we just give up trying to\ndisprove the old man and just settle for the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004180,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact that it's probably all right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006980,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Answer: never."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009140,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's not how science works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010800,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still - nice run, Albert."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013950,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Belodri writes from Austria to inform us that\ntheir phone ring is now me saying Zwicky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020170,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Belodri, please notify us if you're ever coming\nto New York so I can not leave the house."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Randomly hearing that while walking down the\nstreet would creep me out too much."
    } ]
  } ]
}
